# Various IDEs and Editors
.vscode/
.idea/
**/*~

# Mac OSX temporary files
.DS_Store
**/.DS_Store

# dfx temporary files
.dfx/

# icp-cli: cache (local replicas + build cache) is ephemeral and regenerated.
# .icp/data holds the durable mainnet canister-id mapping
# (.icp/data/mappings/ic.ids.json) the CLI reads, so it IS committed.
.icp/cache/

# generated files. icp-cli has no `generate` step (unlike dfx), so the frontend's
# committed Candid bindings under src/declarations must be tracked. Other
# declarations dirs (build artifacts) stay ignored.
**/declarations/
!/src/declarations/
!/src/declarations/**

# rust
target/

# frontend code
node_modules/
dist/
.svelte-kit/
# adapter-static output. Regenerated by every build; never a source of truth, and
# a committed build is how a bundle wired at mainnet gets shipped by accident
# (docs/DEFECTS.md T-01).
# Vite build OUTPUT. Note the negation below: this directory also holds a build
# INPUT (network-env.mjs, the module that stops a bare `npm run build` wiring the
# UI to the live mainnet canisters). Ignoring the whole directory meant a fresh
# clone could not build the frontend at all -- CI caught it on its first real run,
# the third time this repo has shipped a tree that only builds on the machine that
# wrote it.
src/cleardeck_frontend/build/
!src/cleardeck_frontend/build/network-env.mjs

# screenshot evidence. The PNGs and videos are REGENERATED by
# `./scripts/dev.sh shots` against the local replica, and one run is ~10 MiB, so
# they are deliberately not carried in a public git history. What IS committed is
# the evidence ABOUT them: artifacts/screens/**/INDEX.md and verdicts.json record
# every scene, its on-chain assertions, its verdict and the file each shot was
# written to, which is what a reader needs. `make hygiene` fails if the binaries
# ever start being committed.
artifacts/screens/**/*.png
artifacts/screens/**/*.webm
artifacts/screens/**/*.mp4
# ...AND SO IS THE MANIFEST (docs/DEFECTS.md E-60). manifest.json used to be
# tracked on the argument above -- it is "the evidence about the PNGs" -- but it
# is not evidence, it is the working-out: every pixel sample, every occluder
# pair, every classified token. When per-figure occlusion sampling arrived it
# went from 763,703 bytes to 7,612,380 IN ONE WAVE, `make hygiene` went red, and
# ~23 MB of machine-generated JSON no human has ever read was sitting in a public
# history. It stays on disk next to the PNGs it describes, for whoever ran the
# sweep, and it is regenerated by every run.
#
# The tracked evidence is the pair beside it, and BOTH are load-bearing:
#   INDEX.md      the human-readable table (~25 KB)
#   verdicts.json one row per (scene, viewport): the verdict and the headline of
#                 every failure (~11 KB for the run that produced the 7.6 MB
#                 manifest). `./scripts/dev.sh shots-verdict` GATES on this file,
#                 which is what stops a recorded red being walked past (E-63).
artifacts/screens/**/manifest.json
# NOT ignored: artifacts/screens/.thirdparty-cache/*.json. Those are the recorded
# third-party quotes a run replayed or fetched, they are tracked already, and they
# are small text.

# environment variables
.env

# FINDING 42's reproducer output (docs/SECURITY-FINDINGS.md FINDING 42).
# Same policy as artifacts/screens above, and for the same reason: the PNGs and
# the substituted bundle are REGENERATED by `node tools/shots/repro-finding42.mjs`
# against the local replica, and one run writes ~1 MB of screenshots plus a whole
# built frontend. What IS committed is the evidence ABOUT them --
# artifacts/finding42/INDEX.md and the pre-fix/post-fix evidence.json pair, which
# carry the addresses, the rendered strings and the verdict.
artifacts/finding42/**/*.png
artifacts/finding42/dist-substituted/
